{"id":2971,"date":"2021-06-16T06:38:19","date_gmt":"2021-06-16T13:38:19","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=2971"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:39:51","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:39:51","slug":"variables-1-4","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/variables-1-4\/","title":{"rendered":"1.4: Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da74cef\" name=\"6a2214da74cef\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Why are variables useful?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Before we get into variables, you need to understand a situation where they would be useful. Let\u2019s review how to write a program for a square.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Forward, turn left, forward, turn left\u2026<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3228&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-2974 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png\" alt=\"your-turn icon\" width=\"53\" height=\"53\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-150x150.png 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn.png 800w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 53px) 100vw, 53px\" \/><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now make the square bigger by increasing the duration of all of the forwards for 2 seconds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-2974\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png\" alt=\"your-turn icon\" width=\"55\" height=\"55\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-150x150.png 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn.png 800w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 55px) 100vw, 55px\" \/><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now make it even bigger by increasing the duration of all of the forward commands to 3 seconds!<\/span><\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"wp-image-2975 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/think-300x300.png\" alt=\"think icon\" width=\"55\" height=\"55\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/think-300x300.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/think-150x150.png 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/think.png 800w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 55px) 100vw, 55px\" \/><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Phew! That\u2019s a lot of work to change each line of code every time. How do you think we can make this code more efficient?\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da74e05\" name=\"6a2214da74e05\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a variable?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Just like humans keep track of values in our brains, computers keep track of values in their program memory using variables. A variable is a placeholder that is assigned a value. Think of variables as storage containers in your program\u2019s memory. You can create more than 1 variable, and they can hold various different values.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2977&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da74e63\" name=\"6a2214da74e63\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You are going to create a placeholder for the driving duration. Then, you only have to change one line of code to apply the changes throughout our whole code. Open the &#8220;Variables&#8221; menu in Blockly.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2976&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Remember our analogy that variables are like program storage? Variables should have descriptive names so you can easily find that information later. Name this variable &#8220;seconds&#8221;.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2978&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once a variable has been created and assigned a value, you can use it later on in your program. Whenever your program sees the variable name, it will replace it with its value that you assigned. Think of the program as looking into the program storage, finding the correctly labeled package, and checking its value inside.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da74eb5\" name=\"6a2214da74eb5\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Setting a variable value<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Once you create a variable, new blocks will appear in the menu. One sets the value of the variable and one gets the value in the variable from your program storage.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2979&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Before you can use the variable in your program, let&#8217;s set the value to equal 1. First, move the set variable block to the workspace.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2980&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Open the &#8220;Math&#8221; menu and select the first block in the list with the number &#8220;0&#8221;.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2981&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Connect the number block to set the &#8220;seconds&#8221; block to 1. You can change the value in the number block using your keyboard.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2982&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da74f05\" name=\"6a2214da74f05\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Once a variable has been created, you can use it in your program. Replace all of the blocks with the seconds value with the variable. From the &#8220;Variables&#8221; menu, choose the &#8220;get&#8221; blocks to retrieve the variable from memory. Think about &#8220;getting&#8221; the variable from storage![\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3140&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da74f4e\" name=\"6a2214da74f4e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Changing a variable<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Now let&#8217;s make the square bigger. Instead of changing all of the individual forward blocks, now you only have to change one variable! Set the seconds variable to 2 seconds then run the code. What happens?[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3141&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-2974\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png\" alt=\"your-turn icon\" width=\"55\" height=\"55\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-150x150.png 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn.png 800w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 55px) 100vw, 55px\" \/>Practice changing the seconds variable. You can use decimals, too![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da74fb6\" name=\"6a2214da74fb6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating more variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Remember we said you can make more than one variable in a program? Now let\u2019s create a variable so we can simultaneously change the speed value for all of the forward commands. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Since the parameter is also called speed, the variable must have a different name.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-2974\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png\" alt=\"your-turn icon\" width=\"55\" height=\"55\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-150x150.png 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn.png 800w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 55px) 100vw, 55px\" \/>Below the speed variable declaration, create a new variable <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">power<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> and assign it the value 40.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3142&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now you have created a second variable and assigned it to the value 50. Next, you will need to use that variable in your program to set the speed of the forward command.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-2974\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png\" alt=\"your-turn icon\" width=\"55\" height=\"55\" srcset=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-300x300.png 300w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn-150x150.png 150w, https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2021\/06\/your-turn.png 800w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 55px) 100vw, 55px\" \/>In each of the forward commands, set the speed to 40 using the variable <em>power<\/em>.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3143&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Great job! Now you can modify both the speed and duration variables.<\/span><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da75007\" name=\"6a2214da75007\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Changing variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A variable is named after the fact that it can vary, or change. You were changing your variable before running the program, but you can assign the variable a new value in the middle of your program, too.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For example, let the first three sides of the square have a duration of 2. But then change the variable to be 1 so that the last side of the square is smaller. Continue changing the variable to make a square spiral! It will look something like this:<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3144&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]To change the value of a variable in the middle of the program, use the &#8220;set&#8221; variable block. Take a look at what some example code would look like:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;3145&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214da75060\" name=\"6a2214da75060\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Review<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Great job learning all about variables! From now on, you will be using variables in most future lessons. Some variables will store numbers, but others can store words or pictures!<\/p>\n<p>In this lesson, you learned how to:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create and name a variable<\/li>\n<li>Set a variable to a value<\/li>\n<li>Use the variable&#8217;s value in your program<\/li>\n<li>Set the variable to a different value in the middle of the program<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2938&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 Why are variables useful? [vc_column_text]Before we get into variables, you need to understand a situation where they would be useful. Let\u2019s review how to write a program&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/variables-1-4\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2898,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":2878,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2108,2122,2117,2230],"class_list":["post-2971","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-variables","lesson-tag-zumi","module-junior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2971","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":10,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2971\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":11748,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/2971\/revisions\/11748"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2898"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=2971"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=2971"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=2971"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}